# Line endings.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows, where git is configured with
# `core.autocrlf=true`: it stores LF in the object database and checks out CRLF
# on disk. For most files that is invisible and harmless. For these two kinds it
# is not, and it produced a real defect.
#
# ── WHAT IT BROKE (2026-07-28) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `infrastructure/migrations/run.sh` records a sha256 of each migration in
# `public.schema_migrations` and refuses to proceed if an applied migration's
# checksum has changed — the guard that stops the live schema drifting from the
# migration history. It hashed the file on disk.
#
# 001 and 002 were applied while their working-tree copies still had LF, so the
# LF hash went into the ledger. A later checkout rewrote them as CRLF. From that
# moment the guard compared a CRLF hash against an LF one, failed, and reported:
#
#     ERROR: 001_tenancy_foundation already applied but its checksum changed.
#            Write a NEW migration instead of editing an applied one.
#
# Nobody had edited it — `git show HEAD:...` matched the ledger exactly. The
# guard was accusing the wrong thing, and because it exits non-zero on the FIRST
# migration, it blocked every subsequent one. Migration 003 was consequently
# applied by hand and never recorded in the ledger, which is precisely the drift
# the guard exists to prevent: the check meant to stop drift caused it.
#
# `run.sh` now hashes LF-normalised content so the ledger is stable whichever way
# a file is checked out. This file removes the cause rather than only the symptom.
#
# ── SHELL SCRIPTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A CRLF `.sh` is a latent trap of its own: a heredoc terminator carrying a
# trailing \r does not match its opening token, so the heredoc never closes and
# the step silently produces nothing. This project has already lost time to that
# exact failure once.
#
# Scoped deliberately to the file types where line endings change behaviour,
# rather than a repo-wide `* text=auto`, which would rewrite every tracked file
# on a Windows checkout for no benefit.

*.sql text eol=lf
*.sh  text eol=lf
